From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 02:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Safe points, and the x87 stack at a fault

Safe points. An asynchronous signal stopped a thread only when it happened to interrupt it at the first host instruction of a guest
instruction, which loops of loads, indirect jumps, helpers or string instructions hardly ever are. Now a thread that is to stop
does so at safe points, and nowhere else: the entry of a unit and the head of every loop in it, where the guest's state is in the
static registers and the context by the translator's own rules for blocks. A unit's entry stores the address of its header through
CpuStateFrame::SafepointPtr, and a loop's head reads there; a stop is asked for by making that pointer one that faults.
 - REP MOVS and STOS of 32 KiB and more run in parts of 32 KiB, with RCX, RSI and RDI as x86 has them between two. REP CMPS, SCAS
   and LODS stop between two elements.
 - No entry of the RIP table is a boundary to stop at any more (GuestOpcodeFlags::Exact is gone).
 - A loop without a safe point is said when a unit is compiled.
 - The (unused) caches of translated code get another format.

The x87 stack. It was written to the context before every instruction, which converted what was only copied to 80 bits. Now what
is not in the context is kept where the fault handler finds it, as the guest loaded it, before instructions that can fault alone
(IR::X87Pending).
